{"plan_objective_id": 1, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 1,
  "objective": "Research moisture adsorption/condensation on fresh aluminum alloy powder surfaces as a function of RH and temperature",
  "rationale": "The user needs to understand water uptake mechanisms when cold powder meets humid air.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found data on moisture adsorption isotherms for alumina (Type V isotherm, 3-132 mg/g at 70-90% RH), BET water adsorption surface area for Al powder (0.125 m²/g), capillary force effects on flowability. When powder is colder than the dew point, the effective local RH at the surface exceeds 100%, leading to bulk condensation.",
  "evaluation": "Sufficient data gathered from Yang 2020, Yamasaki 2006, Stevens 2009, and others."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 2, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 2,
  "objective": "Research reactivity of fresh vs. passivated Al powder with water vapor, and commercial passivation procedures",
  "rationale": "Fresh atomized Al powder has minimal or no native oxide and may be more reactive with moisture.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Fresh gas-atomized Al powder has ~5 nm oxide. Commercial inert-gas atomizers add 1-2 vol% O2. GARS process evacuates to 6.67 Pa with dew point <-67°C. Anderson 2018 notes controlled O2 addition during spray cooling. Humid-air-exposed powder releases much more H2 during degassing than dry-air-exposed powder. Passivation is primarily in-situ during atomization rather than a separate post-atomization step.",
  "evaluation": "Good evidence from Anderson 2001, Anderson 2018, Yamasaki 2004/2006, Lienhard 2020."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 3, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 3,
  "objective": "Find measured consequences of moisture pickup: oxide/hydroxide formation, hydrogen content, flowability, and LPBF porosity with quantitative data",
  "rationale": "The user needs specific numbers (ppm H, wt% O, porosity %) and DOIs.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Fedina 2022: virgin AlSi10Mg powder 0.067 wt% O, 0.003 wt% H (30 ppm); after 96h aging 0.257 wt% O, 0.003 wt% H. Schulze 2025: 230 ppm H, 305 ppm O in parts; Riener reports 143-908 ppm H, 683-759 ppm O. Van Cauwenbergh: AlSi7Mg0.6 parts 13±4 ppm H, powder 45±11 ppm H. Weingarten: 34 ml/100g H possible, ~50× solubility. LPBF parts show 99.93% to 96.74% density depending on treatment. AlSi10Mg at 80% RH shows large agglomerates, poor flowability. 200°C preheating reduces H porosity ~50%.",
  "evaluation": "Good quantitative data gathered from multiple sources with DOIs."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 4, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 4,
  "objective": "Research drying procedures for aluminum powders and whether they reverse hydroxide formation",
  "rationale": "Need to determine if drying can restore moisture-contaminated powder.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Physisorbed water desorbs below ~175°C. Hydroxide decomposition: Al(OH)3→AlOOH at 150-350°C; AlOOH→Al2O3 at 350-500°C. H2 from hydroxide reactions peaks at ~725K (452°C). Yamasaki recommends 623K (350°C) as optimal degassing temperature. Ludwig 2022: drying at 230°C insufficient for tri-hydroxide decomposition, 300°C for 1h removes bayerite/gibbsite. Drying removes physisorbed water but does NOT reverse hydroxide formation; it converts hydroxides to oxide + H2, keeping the oxide layer. Van Cauwenbergh: laser drying was inefficient for reducing hydrogen pores.",
  "evaluation": "Good evidence from Yamasaki 2006, Zhang 2010, Estrada 1991, Ludwig 2022."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 5, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 5,
  "objective": "Research minimum cooling water temperature relative to dew point for induction furnaces, vacuum furnaces, and atomizers",
  "rationale": "Need manufacturer recommendations and failure modes from condensation.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Gombert 2004 (cold crucible induction melter, INEEL): manufacturer and Russian collaborators specifically recommended maintaining water temperature well above the dew point to prevent condensation on high-voltage RF components, which caused arcing and trips. No specific numerical margin found in open literature. This is a gap area - most guidance is in manufacturer manuals, not peer-reviewed publications. The general engineering rule is cooling water should be at least 3-5°C above the ambient dew point.",
  "evaluation": "Limited peer-reviewed evidence found; this appears to be primarily manufacturer/practitioner knowledge. The Gombert reference provides the clearest published statement."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 6, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 6,
  "objective": "Create summary artifacts and provide final comprehensive answer",
  "rationale": "Synthesize all findings into a well-organized answer with citations.",
  "status": "in-progress",
  "result": null,
  "evaluation": null
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 6, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 6,
  "objective": "Create summary artifacts and provide final comprehensive answer",
  "rationale": "Synthesize all findings into a well-organized answer with citations.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Artifacts created and answer ready for submission.",
  "evaluation": "All objectives completed with sufficient evidence."
}"}

